[CLIENT NAME] commissioned this review to [insert driver, e.g., get an independent picture of its security posture ahead of the NIS2 requirements]. The objectives were to:

• identify the most significant security risks to [CLIENT NAME]'s operations;
• assess the maturity of key security processes and controls;
• provide prioritised, practical recommendations that fit [CLIENT NAME]'s size and resources.

The review covered [insert scope, e.g., the whole organisation, or the IT department and its main business systems]. It was structured around [insert framework, e.g., the control themes of ISO/IEC 27001:2022 Annex A, the NIS2 risk-management measures, or the CIS Critical Security Controls] and carried out through:

• <strong>Interviews</strong> with [insert roles, e.g., the CIO, IT operations, HR and a business system owner];
• <strong>Document review</strong> of [insert documents, e.g., the information security policy, risk assessments, incident procedures];
• <strong>Technical spot checks</strong> of [insert checks, e.g., MFA coverage, patch levels, backup restore logs, the inventory of admin accounts].

<strong>Out of scope:</strong> [insert exclusions]

The campaign was designed to:

• measure how many employees click and submit credentials in response to a realistic phishing email;
• measure how many employees report the email, and how quickly;
• test whether technical controls (email filtering, link protection, MFA) stop or limit the attack;
• provide a baseline for [CLIENT NAME]'s security awareness work.
